{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How does the regional political climate moderate the relationship between individual political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates the relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      "Conservative individuals living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions, and liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives' likelihood was associated with their political climate."
    ],
    "evidence": [
      "683 participants were surveyed between March 8 and April 19, 2023, using an online self-reported study design with data collected through Qualtrics Panels.",
      "Political conservatism was measured using two items assessing social and economic conservatism, averaged into a single scale rated from 0 to 10.",
      "Regional political climate was assessed using zip code data and 2020 presidential election results from the New York Times, with the percentage of Republican votes by zip code serving as a proxy for conservative regional climate.",
      "Vaccine hesitancy was measured using the seven-item Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale, modified to assess COVID-19 vaccine attitudes, and averaged into a scale from 1 to 5.",
      "Booster hesitancy was assessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy."
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      "There was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy (β = .07, p = .03; β = .12, p < .001, respectively), such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate.",
      "Conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
      "A similar interaction was for the likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 booster (OR = .84, p = .049). Liberals were more likely to receive a booster regardless of political climate, while conservatives’ likelihood was associated with their political climate.",
      "For booster status, the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate, while conservatives in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, and in conservative climates less than 35% likely to get boosted (OR = .83, p = .026).",
      "The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate."
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      "The study did not assess the mechanisms through which political climate influences vaccine behavior.",
      "The use of 2020 presidential election votes as a proxy for political climate may be influenced by other zip code-based differences, such as population density.",
      "The study relied on self-reported vaccine status, which is susceptible to social desirability and recall bias.",
      "The sample was recruited through an online platform and includes only White and Black participants, limiting generalizability to broader U.S. populations.",
      "The data is cross-sectional, so causal or directional associations cannot be assumed."
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      "The authors place themselves within the existing literature on political orientation and health behavior, extending prior research by examining the moderating role of regional political climate on the relationship between individual political conservatism and vaccine hesitancy and uptake. They state: 'Our research is consistent with previous findings that conservative political orientation and a conservative political climate are both associated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and decreased likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 vaccine.'"
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      "The paper attributes the claim that 'conservative political orientation and a conservative political climate are both associated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and decreased likelihood of receiving a COVID-19 vaccine' to previous studies, and then builds on it by showing that the interaction between political orientation and regional climate is significant, particularly for booster uptake in liberal regions."
    ]
  }
}
